{
  "title": "The Milky Way over Manhattan",
  "date": "2026-10-15",
  "year": "2026",
  "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> The <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2022-november-12-eclipse-in-the-city/\">city lights</a> outshine the stars in the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_City_That_Never_Sleeps_(nickname)\">City That Never Sleeps</a>. Who would have thought that the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-august-2-fireflies-meteors-and-milky-way/\">Milky Way</a> could be visible in this sky? This <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DeNUSc7u_KQ/\">image</a> was taken with a filter that blocks most <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/09_visiblelight/\">visible light</a>, capturing instead only light in the near-<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/07_infraredwaves/\">infrared</a> region of the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/01_intro/\">electromagnetic spectrum</a> (with lower energy than visible light). The astrophotographer's <a href=\"https://petapixel.com/2026/10/07/photographer-captures-the-milky-way-over-manhattan-despite-the-light-pollution/\">clever setup</a> blocks most of the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-june-26-milky-way-urban-style/\">light pollution</a> from the city: old <a href=\"https://scienceinschool.org/article/2015/starlight-inside-light-bulb/\">incandescent light bulbs</a> and some <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ok__l1Acuwg\">neon lights</a> are still <a href=\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Spectrum-of-the-near-infrared-band-of-a-neon-lamp-detected-by-a-commercial-spectrometer_fig2_302915423\">bright</a> in the near-infrared, but <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/spectrum-of-a-fluorescent-light-bulb-vs-spectrum-of-the-sun/\">fluorescent</a> and <a href=\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Emission-spectra-of-different-light-sources-a-incandescent-tungsten-light-bulb-b_fig1_312320039\">LED</a> light bulbs are not. Of course, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/universe/stars/\">stars</a> are also bright in the near-infrared! This image is a reminder that we all live <a href=\"https://iauarchive.eso.org/news/announcements/detail/ann20019/\">under the same sky</a>, and astronomy allows all of us to learn more about <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uLu1cTKBspI\">humanity's place in the Cosmos</a>. <br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>Chasing the ISS",
